An unordered, forward-facing backlog: what is still to do, plus the negative
findings that would otherwise be paid for twice. Completed work is not logged
here - its history lives in docs/plans/, docs/design/, and git.

Items are listed alphabetically; the order carries no meaning. An item
graduates to its own docs/plans/<name>.md when it is picked up, and the
planning/implementation/review process is docs/plans/README.md.

Seeded 2026-07-27 from the state of the initial import; prune freely.

items
  estimand-surface: irt_causal_bart() returns only the population ATE,
    accumulated per scan. The per-person f(1, theta) - f(0, theta)
    surface is already computed and discarded, so CATE/ICATE draws and
    subgroup estimands are nearly free at the cost of an n x draws
    matrix. Wants the same opt-in treatment keep_theta gets, or the
    per-draw reducing-callback shape stan4bart has scoped for the same
    problem (stan4bart TODO "callback-estimators"). consumer-gated.

  effective-sample-size: irt_rhat() reports R-hat but nothing about how
    much information the draws carry, so there is no way to tell a
    converged-but-autocorrelated run from an efficient one, and no basis
    for choosing n_sampling. Wants bulk and tail ESS over the same
    draws-by-chains arrays irt_chain_draws() already returns; the
    machinery (split, rank-normalize, fold) is in R/chains.R and would be
    shared. Deliberately left out of multiple-chains, which scoped to the
    between-chain diagnostic only - see docs/plans/multiple-chains.md,
    "Constraints". Needs an FFT autocovariance and Geyer's initial
    monotone sequence, so it is real numerical code, not a wrapper.

  theta-block-move: dbarts::updatePredictorPerObservationJointly() takes
    a SINGLE shared column by design (it errors on more; see the
    function's own guard). multi-trait therefore landed with a
    component-wise systematic scan - update trait k for all persons,
    then trait k+1 - which is a valid Metropolis-within-Gibbs sweep but
    cannot move a person's whole trait vector as a unit. A genuine block
    move needs multi-column support in dbarts. Mixing at K = 2 does not
    yet motivate it: on the motivating DGP (1000 persons, 100 items per
    bank) the scan holds ~0.44 acceptance and recovers both banks at
    cor(theta) 0.975, so there is no measured cost to pay dbarts for.
    Revisit if K grows or the traits are ever given a correlated prior,
    where component-wise moves would fight the correlation.
    consumer-gated.
